          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong quality type
            is a key concept in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W.+Lawvere"} W. Lawvere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            which permits to analyze a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            understood as a domain of
            %em quantitative
            variation via its
            %em qualitative
            aspects. This can be viewed as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatisation
            of the commonly encountered situation in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            that problems permit (only) a qualitative analysis.
          %p
            A primary example for that kind of deferred analysis is the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            (hence via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            which they represent). So in a very broad sense, quality types are intended as ingredients to a
            %em synthetic homotopy theory
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            where the (homotopy) contraction of a space (the collapsing of a
            = succeed "/two" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder+object"} cylinder
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotents"} idempotents
          %p
            Technically, a quality type amounts to a special sort of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential localization
            and is therefore called a
            %strong quintessential localization
            in (
            %a{:href => "#JS96"}> Johnstone 1996
            ).
          %p
            In typical (topos) cases these come in the form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modalities"} adjoint cylinders
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi\dashv\Delta\dashv\Gamma
            (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section"} global sections functor
            coincide (=“collapse”):
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi\cong\Gamma
            (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points-to-pieces+transform"} points-to-pieces transform
            %strong infinitesimal spaces
            %sup#fnref:2
              %a{:href => "#fn:2", :rel => "footnote"} 2
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Law08"}> Lawvere 2008
            ).
          %p
            Hence from a more
            = succeed "," do
              %em geometrical point of view
            %em almost
            no cohesion.
            %sup#fnref:3
              %a{:href => "#fn:3", :rel => "footnote"} 3
            (For an elaboration of this perspective in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+toposes"} cohesive toposes
            see at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+cohesion"} infinitesimal cohesion
          %p
            Quality types together with the
            %strong continuity axiom
            are an essential ingredient to Lawvere’s (
            %a{:href => "#Law07"}> 2007 axioms
            ) for geometry:
          %blockquote
            %p
              That Continuity axiom (preservation of infinite products by
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
              %sup#fnref:4
                %a{:href => "#fn:4", :rel => "footnote"} 4
              (as they should be automatically in the continuous case). Lawvere (
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/punkdit/categories/blob/master/gmane/science/mathematics/categories/7016"}> message to catlist oct 26th 2011
              )
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Law98"}> Lawvere 1998
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Law08"} Lawvere 2008
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#MarmoMenni16"} Marmolejo-Menni 2016
            %em pure variation
            and
            %em sufficient cohesion
            permitting to attend to the
            %em fine structure
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} petit-gros
            landscape. So we can say with only slight exaggeration that the 2007 axioms revolve around the concept of a quality type and that cohesive spaces are those that admit qualitative (homotopical) analysis!
          %p
            In the following we discuss the case for ordinary categories, for the higher order generalization see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} infinitesimal cohesive (infinity,1)-topos
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S},\mathcal{F}
            be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+categories"} extensive categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^*:\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{F}
            is said to exhibit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            as a
            %strong quality type
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^*
            has both a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            which moreover coincide (an
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambidextrous+adjoint"} ambidextrous adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!\dashv q^*\dashv q_*
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo !
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!\cong q_*
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            be a (pre)cohesive category over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            with adjoint string
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_!\dashv p^* \dashv p_* \dashv p^!:\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{E}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^*:\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{F}
            a quality type over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{F}
            is called an
            %strong intensive quality
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            preserves finite products and finite coproducts and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_*\circ s =p_*
          %p
            Dually, a finite coproduct preserving functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{F}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo !
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!\circ h=p_!
            is called an
            %strong extensive quality
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %h4#remark Remark
          %p Intuitively, an intensive quality is compatible with the points of its domain spaces and an extensive quality with the connected components.
          %p
            Note that a quality type yields itself a (pre)cohesive category with (degenerate) adjoint string
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!\dashv q^*\dashv q_*\dashv q^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_*\to p_!
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nullstellensatz"} Nullstellensatz
            is not only an epimorphism but a natural isomorphism (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Law07"} Lawvere 2007
          %h2#examples Examples
          %blockquote
            %p If we divest smooth spaces of all global cohesion, keeping only the jets (on which the Thom-Mather singularities depend), we obtain a category in which every connected component of any object has exactly one point, so that the natural map between those functors is an isomorphism. Lawvere (2004, p.108)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Kan complexes over
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#MarmoMenni16"} 2016
            %li
              %p Galois theory - presheaves on the opposite of the category of finite-dimensional local k-algebras (Lawvere 2004).
          %h4#remark_2 Remark
          %p
            The properties of (pre)cohesive categories are fine-tuned to yield a canonical extensive quality via the Hurewicz construction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi (X^Y)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GabrielZisman67"} 1967
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+space"} Kelley spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fractions"} category of fractions
            necessary in this case.
          %h3#TheToposOfF1Actions
            The topos of
            = succeed "-actions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1
          %p
            Lawvere’s work on the petit-gros topos distinction (
            %a{:href => "#Law76"}> Lawvere 1976
            ) started with the examination of two toposes of graphs (
            %a{:href => "#Law86"}> Lawvere 1986
            ). The first one of
            %em
              “irreflexive”
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} graphs
            is given by the presheaf topos of actions of the small diagram category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\rightrightarrows V
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            and is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendue"} étendue
            hence petit.
          %p
            Whereas the second one of
            %em reflexive graphs
            is given by the actions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphic+monoid"} graphic monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta _1=\{1,\partial_0,\partial_1\}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_i\partial_j=\partial_i
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i,j=0,1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta _1
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
            to the diagram category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ⇆
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\stackrel{\leftrightarrows}{\leftarrow} V
            and consists entirely of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotents"} idempotents
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet
            satisfies the axioms for a (sufficiently)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta _1
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1=\{1,e\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            which incidentally is also the monoid underlying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blueprint"} blueprint
            for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+with+one+element"} field with one element
          %p
            As observed in (
            %a{:href => "#Law89"}> Lawvere 1989
            , p.277)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝔽
                        %mn 1
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\mathbb{F}_1^{op}}
            is a quality type over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
          %p
            The surjective homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:\Delta_1\to\mathbb{F}_1
            induces an adjoint string
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 1
                        %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝔽
                        %mn 1
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!\dashv q^*\dashv q_*:\mathcal{S}^{\Delta_1^{op}}\to\mathcal{S}^{\mathbb{F}_1^{op}}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_*
            discarding all non-loops in a reflexive graph.
          %p
            For details how the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function"} zeta function
            arises via the Burnside ring of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝔽
                        %mn 1
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\mathbb{F}_1^{op}}
            in this context see (
            %a{:href => "#Law89"}> Lawvere 1989
            , pp.291-292).
            = succeed "-torsors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#J02a"} 2002
          %h2#quality_types_as_localizations Quality types as localizations
          %p
            Properties of quality types were first studied by P. Johnstone (
            %a{:href => "#JS96"}> 1996
            ) using the language of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localizations
          #quintessentialloc.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a finitely complete category. An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level"} essential localization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi r
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l\dashv r\dashv i:\mathcal{L}\to\mathcal{C}
              is called
              %strong quintessential
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l
              is naturally isomorphic to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %p
            A trivial example of a quintessential localization is provided by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_\mathcal{C}
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Another simple
              %em example
              of a quintessential localization is given by the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              with objects pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, e)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a set and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e=e^2
              an idempotent map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:(X_1, e_1)\to (X_2, e_2)
              is a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X_1\to X_2
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⋅
