                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\cdot r\dashv i\cdot r:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{C}
              corresponding to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi r
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\dashv r\dashv i:Set\to\mathcal{C}
              map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,e)
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r(X),id_{r(X)})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is up to equivalence just the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒮
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi 𝔽
                          %mn 1
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\mathbb{F}_1^{op}}
              from above!
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Consider the monoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\overline{\mathbb{N}}, \cdot , 0)
              consisting of the natural numbers in their usual order with a point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              attached at infinity such that
              = succeed "<" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo :
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall n\in \mathbb{N}: n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              with multiplication
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mi max
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi m
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\cdot n=max\{m,n\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathbb{N}}
              is commutative and satisfies the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphic+category"} graphic identity
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\cdot y\cdot x = x\cdot y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mover
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:Set\hookrightarrow Set^{\overline{\mathbb{N}}}
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mover
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(X)=(X, \pi_2:\overline{\mathbb{N}}\times X\to X)
              exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mover
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\overline{\mathbb{N}}}
              as a quality type over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              (Cf. Freyd et al. (
              %a{:href => "#FOPTST99"}> 1999
              ) and O’Hearn et al. (
              %a{:href => "#OPTT95"}> 1995
              )).
            %p
              This can be viewed as a more elaborate version of the preceding example where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              corresponds to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              upon identifying
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              as the category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒮
                        %mrow
                          %msubsup
                            %mi 𝔽
                            %mn 1
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\mathbb{F}_1^{op}}
            %p (Caveat: this needs checking!)
          %p
            To say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi r
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l\dashv r\dashv i:\mathcal{L}\to\mathcal{C}
            is a quintessential localization amounts to say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:\mathcal{L}\to\mathcal{C}
            exhibits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            as a quality type over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            providing the right adjoint to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\simeq l
            (provided
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℒ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            are extensive).
          %p
            Note that a quintessential subtopos is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subtopos"} dense
            since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is up to natural isomorphism a left adjoint whence preserves all colimits and the initial object in particular!
          %p
            Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modalities
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi r
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi i
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l\cdot r\dashv i\cdot r
            corresponding to a quintessential subtopos as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level
            coincide up to natural isomorphism, one sees that quintessential levels are their own
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
          #persistentloc.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a finitely complete category. A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\dashv i:\mathcal{L}\to\mathcal{C}
              is called
              %strong persistent
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℒ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}
              is closed under subobjects in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            Quintessential localizations of toposes are persistent (
            %a{:href => "#JS96"}> Johnstone 1996
            , p.94). Since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+object"} separated objects
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topology"} Lawvere-Tierney topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            are precisely the subobjects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                  %mo −
                  %mi sheaves
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j-sheaves
            it follows that a persistent (and, in particular, a quintessential) subtopos coincides with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos"} quasitopos
            of
            = succeed "-separated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
          #quintidempotent.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+completion"} Cauchy- complete category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              correspond precisely to idempotent endomorphisms of the identity functor on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              form a semilattice under intersection.
          %p
            For the proof cf. (
            %a{:href => "#JS96"}> Johnstone 1996
            , p.93).
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} infinitesimal cohesive (infinity,1)-topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+space"} compactly generated topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+localization"} essential localization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bireflective+subcategory"} bireflective subcategory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambidextrous+adjunction"} ambidextrous adjunction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nullstellensatz"} Nullstellensatz
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
          %h2#remarks_on_the_literature Remarks on the literature
          %p
            The definition of quality types occurs in (
            %a{:href => "#Law07"}> Lawvere 2007
            ) with important additional remarks in (
            %a{:href => "#Law08"}> Lawvere 2008
            ). The first occurrence of a quality type is in the work of the 1980s in the context of the gros topos of graphs: a discussion of this example is in (
            %a{:href => "#Law89a"}> Lawvere 1989
            , p.277) where already the contrast to sufficient cohesion is observed. (
            %a{:href => "#Law91a"}> Lawvere 1991
            , pp.9-10) has a short but suggestive discussion in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Law98"} Lawvere 1998
            %a{:href => "#Law04"} 2004 paper
            on data types.
          %p
            Under the name of quintessential localization they are the focus of (
            %a{:href => "#JS96"}> Johnstone 1996
            ) where the characterization via central idempotents is given. Some additional results occur in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nullstellensatz"} Nullstellensatz
            in (
            %a{:href => "#JS11"}> Johnstone 2011
            ). (
            %a{:href => "#Menni14b"}> Menni 2014
            ) attends to the contrast between quality types and sufficient cohesion. A variant is studied as the concept of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bireflective+subcategory"} bireflective subcategory
            in Freyd et al. (
            %a{:href => "#FOPTST99"}> 1999
            ).
