          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  The term
                  %em synthetic homotopy theory
                  is slightly misleading here as Lawvere’s ideas don’t provide a fully fledged homotopy theory but are rather observations on the role of the cylinder configuration in the analysis of space and spatial dimension. Important facets of his views are documented in Lawvere
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Law92"} 1992
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Law94"} 1994
                  %a{:href => "#1999"} 1999
                  and a more thorough discussion of the homotopical apects of the concept appears in Marmolejo-Menni (
                  %a{:href => "#MarmoMenni16"}> 2016
                  ). In a complementary direction,
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                  provides a synthetic formulation of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
                  proper, not just of the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
                  implements
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
                  %em in
                  that natively homotopy-theoretic context.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  A motivation for this terminology from
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} SDG
                  can be found in a remark in (
                  %a{:href => "Law91a"}> Lawvere 1991
                  , pp.9-10).
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:3
                %p
                  From another perspective, one could view an object in a quality type as a set with
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em germs of cohesion
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:3", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:4
                %p
                  The claim that
                  %em quality types
                  intend to model the philosophical concept of
                  %em quality
                  reoccurs at several places in Lawvere’s writings though the concrete connection still needs to be spelled out. Some motivation is provided in
                  %a{:href => "#Law92"} Lawvere (1992)
                  where it is linked to the negation of
                  %em quantity
                  as a logical category of being that is indifferent to non-being (cf. Hegel’s
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
                  %em Logic
                  reversed the traditional hierarchy of the categories.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:4", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
